[61.317235, "o", "Done. Three files created and verified in `/app`:\r\n\r\n**`active_users_report.json`** — 346 active users (matches the input's active count), all dates as `YYYY-MM-DD`, keys exactly `user_id, username, email, last_login, role_count, primary_role`, sorted ascending by `username`, 2-space indent.\r\n\r\n**`cmd.sh`**\r\n```\r\njq '[.[] | select(.status == \"active\") | {user_id: .id, username: .username, email: .email, last_login: (.last_login | sub(\"\\\\.[0-9]+\"; \"\") | fromdateiso8601 | strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d\")), role_count: (.roles | length), primary_role: (.roles[0] // null)}] | sort_by(.username)' /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json\r\n```\r\n\r\n**`jq_args.yaml`** — same filter string; `args: []` because no jq flags are needed (2-space indent is jq's default).\r\n\r\nNotes:\r\n- The timestamp is stripped of fractional seconds before `fromdateiso8601`, since jq 1.6 rejects `2026-03-30T15:06:40.723617Z` with that function otherwise.\r\n- `primary_role: (.roles[0] // null)` yields real JSON `null` for the 50 active users with "]
[61.317437, "o", "empty `roles` (verified: 50 zero-role records, 50 `null` primary roles, 0 mismatches).\r\n\r\nVerification run: `bash cmd.sh` and the reconstructed `jq $(yq -r '.args[]' jq_args.yaml) \"$(yq -r '.filter' jq_args.yaml)\" /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json` produced byte-identical output (`diff` clean), and jq checks confirmed valid date format, exact key set, correct `role_count` types, and sort order.\r\n"]
